.TH smi-pki-export-client-pkcs12 "1" "September 2026" "SMI Toolset" "SMI PKI Command"
.SH NAME
smi-pki-export-client-pkcs12 \- Bundle an issued client certificate into a PKCS#12 file
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B smi-pki-export-client-pkcs12
.I DIRECTORY CLIENT_NAME ROOT_DOMAIN
.SH DESCRIPTION
.PP
Bundles \fIDIRECTORY\fB/\fICLIENT_NAME\fB.client.priv.key\fR, \fB.client.crt\fR and the Intermediate
CA \fBca/\fIROOT_DOMAIN\fB.intermediate.crt\fR into \fICLIENT_NAME\fB.client.p12\fR, mode \fB600\fR,
under the alias \fICLIENT_NAME\fR.
.PP
It is the CA side form of \fBsmi-pki-export-pkcs12\fR(1) with the client file naming, for clients
whose key the CA side created with \fBsmi-pki-create-client-cert\fR(1), which runs this command
itself. A client that made its own key and received only a certificate uses
\fBsmi-pki-export-pkcs12\fR(1) instead, because its private key never reached the CA side.
.PP
The export password is taken from
.SM SMI_PKI_PKCS12_PASSWORD
when set, never from the command line; without it OpenSSL asks on the terminal.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B SMI_PKI_PKCS12_PASSWORD
Export password of the PKCS#12 file. Unset means asking on the terminal.
.SH EXAMPLE
.RS
.nf
SMI_PKI_PKCS12_PASSWORD=secret \\
    smi-pki-export-client-pkcs12 /var/opt/pki intranet-bot gintra
.fi
.RE
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR smi-pki-export-pkcs12 (1),
.BR smi-pki-create-client-cert (1),
.BR smi-pki-do-client-signing (1),
.BR openssl (1)
